{"chunk_id": "ada76e40be0c", "text": "Sringin tells his father, the sage Samika, that in anger he has cursed King Parikshit: within seven days the serpent Takshaka will kill him. Samika rebukes his son, teaching that ascetics must practice restraint and forgiveness. He argues that kings, even when they err, uphold social order, protect religious rites, and sustain the cosmic cycle that benefits all. Anger, he warns, destroys the spiritual merit gained through penance. Although the curse cannot be undone because of Sringin\u2019s truthful power, Samika compassionately sends his disciple Gaurmukha to warn the king. When Parikshit hears the message, he feels deep remorse\u2014not primarily for his impending death, but for the disrespect he showed the silent sage. The passage contrasts youthful wrath with mature wisdom and highlights repentance, duty, and the fragile balance between spiritual power and moral restraint.\nAfter cursing King Parikshit, Sringin is rebuked by his father Samika, who warns the king through a messenger; Parikshit repents upon hearing the curse.\nThemes: anger vs forgiveness, duty of kings, ascetic discipline, curse and fate, repentance", "vector": [0.024150477722287178, -0.002104580169543624, -0.003869060194119811, 0.0031417172867804766, -0.0011103764409199357, 0.017119497060775757, -0.018291326239705086, -0.018628060817718506, -0.00175942899659276, -0.023625174537301064, 0.02229171246290207, 0.004185589030385017, 0.015610934235155582, 0.011677894741296768, 0.0009739996748976409, 0.0056604789569973946, 0.057540904730558395, -0.005475275684148073, 0.020917844027280807, -0.005542622413486242, -0.006525882054120302, 0.008532810024917126, -0.0069030229933559895, -0.00295819784514606, -0.02054070308804512, 0.026736587285995483, -0.001128896838054061, -0.02309987135231495, -0.0009310662862844765, -0.02909371629357338, -0.03035983256995678, -0.01602848246693611, -0.0017459597438573837, -0.011664425022900105, -0.044287100434303284, 0.012701562605798244